:\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[4] = 0x40\",\"size\":4},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"cookie_num = 5\",\"size\":8}],\"calls\":[\"setvbuf\",\"alarm\",\"fopen\",\"strdup\",\"_abort\"]},{\"name\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1399\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"_abort\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"calloc\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"size\",\"source\":\"scanf(%lld)\",\"type\":\"int64\"},{\"name\":\"data\",\"source\":\"read(0, buf, size)\",\"type\":\"raw_bytes\"}],\"checks\":[\"cookie_num <= 0x1f (max 32 cookies)\",\"size <= 0x100 (max 256 bytes)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"ALLOC\",\"destination\":\"buf = calloc(size, 1)\",\"size\":\"user-controlled (<=0x100)\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"buf\",\"size\":\"user-controlled (<=0x100)\",\"via\":\"read(0, buf, size)\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[cookie_num] = buf\",\"size\":8},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[cookie_num] = (int32)size\",\"size\":4},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"cookie_num += 1\",\"size\":8}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"calloc\",\"read\",\"puts\"]},{\"name\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x14e1\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"_abort\",\"fread\",\"printf\",\"free\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"random_index\",\"source\":\"fread(/dev/urandom) % cookie_num\",\"type\":\"int32\"}],\"checks\":[\"cookie_num != 0 else _abort\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[random_index]\",\"size\":8,\"used_as\":\"printf argument (%s)\"},{\"operation\":\"FREE\",\"target\":\"msg[random_index]\",\"description\":\"free(msg[random_index])\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1]\",\"size\":8,\"description\":\"swap last element into freed slot\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[random_index] = msg_size[cookie_num-1]\",\"size\":4},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"cookie_num -= 1\",\"size\":8}],\"calls\":[\"fread\",\"printf\",\"free\"],\"notes\":\"When random_index == cookie_num-1, the swap writes msg[cookie_num-1] back into itself (self-assignment), so the freed pointer remains in msg[cookie_num-1] which is now outside the valid range [0, cookie_num-2]. The pointer is not explicitly NULLed.\"},{\"name\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1628\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"index\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"uint64\"},{\"name\":\"data\",\"source\":\"read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\",\"type\":\"raw_bytes\"}],\"checks\":[\"index < cookie_num (signed JL comparison at 0x1681; NO lower-bound check)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg_size[index]\",\"size\":4,\"used_as\":\"read length = msg_size[index] - 1\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[index]\",\"size\":8,\"used_as\":\"write destination pointer\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[index] (dereferenced heap pointer)\",\"size\":\"msg_size[index]-1\",\"via\":\"read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"read\",\"puts\"],\"notes\":\"Index is read as uint64 via %llu but compared with signed JL. Negative values (e.g. large uint64 like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) pass the check since JL is signed. The index is then used with signed LEA scaling (rax*8) and signed LEA for msg_size (rax*4), allowing out-of-bounds access to memory before msg[] and msg_size[].\"},{\"name\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1751\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"index\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"uint64\"}],\"checks\":[\"index < cookie_num (signed JL comparison at 0x17aa; NO lower-bound check)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[index]\",\"size\":8,\"used_as\":\"printf argument with format %s\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\"],\"notes\":\"Same signed JL index check as edit_cookie. Negative/large uint64 index passes check. msg[index] is dereferenced and printed as a string, enabling potential OOB read.\"},{\"name\":\"menu\",\"address\":\"0x17fd\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"puts\"]},{\"name\":\"_abort\",\"address\":\"0x11f5\",\"callers\":[\"init\",\"create_cookie\",\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"msg\",\"source\":\"rdi\",\"type\":\"string\"}],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"]}],\"attack_surfaces\":[{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index (scanf %llu)\",\"operation\":\"WRITE to msg[index] with size msg_size[index]-1\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Attacker-controlled index has NO lower-bound check (only signed JL against cookie_num). Negative index reaches OOB memory before msg[] and msg_size[] arrays. The OOB msg_size value controls read length, and the OOB msg value is dereferenced as the write destination. This gives attacker-controlled arbitrary write primitive if suitable OOB values exist.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index (scanf %llu)\",\"operation\":\"READ msg[index] and printf(%s, msg[index])\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Same missing lower-bound check on index. OOB msg[index] is dereferenced and printed as string, enabling potential info leak from arbitrary memory if a valid pointer can be reached at a negative offset.\"},{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_index (urandom % cookie_num)\",\"operation\":\"FREE msg[random_index] then swap-with-last\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"When random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is self-assigned back (not NULLed), leaving a dangling pointer. However, index is random (not attacker-controlled), so triggering the specific case requires probability. After free, cookie_num decrements, and the dangling slot is at index cookie_num (now out of valid range for edit/read, but the swap logic itself may have issues).\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"message_size (scanf %lld)\",\"operation\":\"ALLOC calloc(size,1) + read(0, buf, size)\",\"risk\":\"LOW\",\"reason\":\"Size is checked <= 0x100. calloc zero-initializes. No obvious overflow. Size stored as int32 in msg_size[].\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is PIE-enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary, not stripped, amd64\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is glibc 2.39 (Ubuntu 2.39-0ubuntu8.8) at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg[] is a pointer array of 32 elements (8 bytes each) at BSS 0x4060, size 256 bytes\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size[] is an int32 array of 32 elements at BSS 0x4160, size 128 bytes\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is int64 at BSS 0x41e8, initialized to 5 by init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() pre-allocates 5 cookies via strdup with sizes 0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num <= 0x1f and size <= 0x100, uses calloc(size,1) then read(0,buf,size)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu) at 0x1671, compares with signed JL at 0x1681 (cmp rdx,rax; jl). No lower-bound (>=0) check exists.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie uses index to read msg_size[index] (at 0x16a7, lea rdx,[rax*4] + msg_size base) as read length minus 1, and msg[index] (at 0x16c3, lea rcx,[rax*8] + msg base) as write destination for read(0, ptr, len)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu) at 0x179a, compares with signed JL at 0x17aa. No lower-bound check. Uses msg[index] as printf %s argument.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie selects random index via fread(/dev/urandom) % cookie_num, frees msg[idx], then copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx] and msg_size[cookie_num-1] into msg_size[idx], decrements cookie_num\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie does not NULL the freed pointer when idx == cookie_num-1 (self-assignment case); the slot at old cookie_num-1 retains the freed pointer value but is now outside [0, new_cookie_num-1]\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: stdout(0x4020,8) stdin(0x4030,8) stderr(0x4040,8) gap(0x4048-0x405f) msg[32](0x4060-0x415f) msg_size[32](0x4160-0x41df) urandom(0x41e0,8) cookie_num(0x41e8,8)\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"scanf format for menu choice is %lld (int64), for edit/read index is %llu (uint64)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) is called in init(), setting a 60-second timeout\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie index check uses signed JL (jump if less, signed). A uint64 value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (-1 signed) passes the check since -1 < cookie_num (5). The index is then used in LEA with rax*8/rax*4 scaling, computing addresses before msg[] base.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"With negative index in edit_cookie, msg_size[neg] reads from memory before 0x4160 (i.e., within msg[] array or earlier BSS), and msg[neg] reads from memory before 0x4060 (i.e., stderr/stdin/stdout pointers or earlier). The read size and write destination are both attacker-influenced via OOB.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"With negative index in read_cookie, msg[neg] reads a pointer from before 0x4060 and passes it to printf(%s), potentially leaking memory content if the OOB value is a valid pointer.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether CET (IBT/SHSTK) is enabled at runtime\",\"reason\":\"checksec does not report CET flags; kernel/config dependent\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether seccomp or other sandbox is active\",\"reason\":\"No seccomp-related symbols or prctl calls found in binary, but external sandbox cannot be ruled out from static analysis alone\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Exact heap layout and tcache behavior under glibc 2.39 for the specific allocation sizes used (0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40 and user-controlled <=0x100)\",\"reason\":\"Requires dynamic heap analysis to determine chunk alignment and tcache bin placement\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie self-assignment dangling pointer is practically reachable and exploitable given the random index\",\"reason\":\"Index is random from /dev/urandom; triggering idx==cookie_num-1 requires 1/N probability per call, needs dynamic verification\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 LSB PIE, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, BuildID=84dab319bf6fe22134cf0338cbd8071fd8bc06c2, not stripped. Entry=0x1110. 11 program headers, 29 sections.\",\"sections\":\".text(0x1110,0x891,AX) .rodata(0x2000,0x266,A) .data(0x4000,0x10,WA) .bss(0x4020,0x1d0,WA) .got(0x3f58,0xa8,WA) .plt(0x1020,0xe0,AX) .init_array(0x3d58,0x8,WA) .fini_array(0x3d60,0x8,WA) .dynamic(0x3d68,0x1f0,WA)\",\"symbols\":\"main(0x187f) init(0x1232) create_cookie(0x1399) eat_cookie(0x14e1) edit_cookie(0x1628) read_cookie(0x1751) menu(0x17fd) _abort(0x11f5). Globals: msg(0x4060,256) msg_size(0x4160,128) cookie_num(0x41e8,8) urandom(0x41e0,8) stdout(0x4020) stdin(0x4030) stderr(0x4040)\",\"disassembly\":\"edit_cookie: scanf(%llu,&idx) at 0x1671; cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692 at 0x1681 (NO lower bound); msg_size[idx] read at 0x16a7 (lea rdx,[rax*4]+0x4160); msg[idx] read at 0x16c3 (lea rcx,[rax*8]+0x4060); read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1) at 0x16de. read_cookie: scanf(%llu,&idx) at 0x179a; cmp rdx,rax; jl at 0x17aa (NO lower bound); printf(%s,msg[idx]) at 0x17e1. eat_cookie: fread(urandom) at 0x152b; idiv cookie_num at 0x153e; free(msg[idx]) at 0x158e; msg[idx]=msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15c6; msg_size[idx]=msg_size[cookie_num-1] at 0x15fc; cookie_num-- at 0x160a. create_cookie: cookie_num<=0x1f check at 0x13b7; size<=0x100 check at 0x13f6; calloc(size,1) at 0x1416; read(0,buf,size) at 0x1443; msg[cookie_num]=buf at 0x1489; msg_size[cookie_num]=size at 0x14a9; cookie_num++ at 0x14c3.\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":4,\"high_severity\":3,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_EDIT_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Index Validation Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads the cookie index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") as a uint64, then checks `index < cookie_num` using a signed JL (jump-if-less) comparison at 0x1681. There is no lower-bound (>=0) check. A large uint64 value (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = -1 signed) passes the signed comparison since -1 < 5. The negative index is then used in LEA scaling (rax*8 for msg[], rax*4 for msg_size[]) to compute addresses before the array bases, enabling out-of-bounds access to memory preceding msg[] and msg_size[] in BSS.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[] (0x4060, 32 pointers)\",\"msg_size[] (0x4160, 32 int32)\",\"stdout (0x4020)\",\"stdin (0x4030)\",\"stderr (0x4040)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") in edit_cookie (menu choice 3)\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\", &index) at 0x1671 reads uint64 index\",\"cmp rdx, rax; jl at 0x1681 — signed comparison, no lower-bound check\",\"lea rdx, [rax*4] + msg_size base at 0x16a7 — OOB read of msg_size[neg] as read length\",\"movsxd rdx, eax at 0x16bc — sign-extends (msg_size[neg]-1) to 64-bit\",\"lea rcx, [rax*8] + msg base at 0x16c3 — OOB read of msg[neg] as write destination pointer\",\"read(0, msg[neg], msg_size[neg]-1) at 0x16de — writes attacker data to OOB-derived pointer\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as unsigned: 0 <= index < cookie_num, using both lower-bound and upper-bound checks with unsigned comparison (JB/JAE).\",\"actual\":\"Only `cmp rdx, rax; jl` (signed less-than) at 0x1681 checks upper bound. No lower-bound check exists. Signed JL allows negative values (large uint64) to pass.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0). Signed comparison JL instead of unsigned JB for upper bound.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array read + pointer dereference + heap write\",\"address\":\"0x16a7 (msg_size OOB read), 0x16c3 (msg OOB read), 0x16de (read to OOB pointer)\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) where index is negative\",\"target\":\"msg[neg] dereferences an OOB pointer from BSS (e.g. stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* at negative offsets from msg base 0x4060)\",\"size\":\"msg_size[neg]-1 (OOB-derived, sign-extended to 64-bit)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"With a negative index, msg[neg] reads a pointer from BSS memory before msg[] (e.g. stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers at offsets -8, -6, -4). This pointer is used as the write destination for read(). If the OOB pointer value is a valid writable address (e.g. a libc FILE* struct), attacker-controlled data is written to that location, causing memory corruption.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"msg_size[neg] reads a 32-bit value from BSS before msg_size[] base (0x4160), which falls within the msg[] pointer array. This OOB value (a truncated heap pointer) controls the read length, and msg[neg] reads an OOB pointer value, both constituting out-of-bounds reads from BSS.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The combination of OOB-derived write destination and OOB-derived write length allows corruption of memory at the location pointed to by the OOB pointer (e.g. FILE structures in libc).\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx, rax; jl 0x1692 — signed JL comparison on uint64 index. No lower-bound (>=0) check. Negative values (large uint64) pass the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7\",\"description\":\"lea rdx, [rax*4+0x0] then add msg_size base (0x4160) — uses negative index to compute OOB address before msg_size[] array, reading an OOB int32 as the read length.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3\",\"description\":\"lea rcx, [rax*8+0x0] then add msg base (0x4060) — uses negative index to compute OOB address before msg[] array, reading an OOB pointer as the write destination for read().\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16bc\",\"description\":\"movsxd rdx, eax — sign-extends (msg_size[neg]-1) to 64-bit. If OOB msg_size value is 0, result is 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg[] at 0x4060, stdout at 0x4020 (offset -8 from msg), stdin at 0x4030 (offset -6), stderr at 0x4040 (offset -4). Negative indices -8, -6, -4 reach these FILE* pointers, which are valid writable libc addresses.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that OOB FILE* pointers (stdout/stdin/stderr) are valid writable addresses at runtime under ASLR\",\"Verify the exact write length when msg_size[neg] is a truncated heap pointer value\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_READ_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Index Validation Error\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads the cookie index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") as a uint64, then checks `index < cookie_num` using a signed JL comparison at 0x17aa. There is no lower-bound (>=0) check. A large uint64 value (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = -1 signed) passes the check. The negative index is used in LEA scaling (rax*8) to read an OOB pointer from before msg[] base, which is then dereferenced and printed as a string via printf(\\\"%s\\\").\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[] (0x4060, 32 pointers)\",\"stdout (0x4020)\",\"stdin (0x4030)\",\"stderr (0x4040)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") in read_cookie (menu choice 4)\",\"entry_function\":\"read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\", &index) at 0x179a reads uint64 index\",\"cmp rdx, rax; jl at 0x17aa — signed comparison, no lower-bound check\",\"lea rdx, [rax*8] + msg base at 0x17bf — OOB read of msg[neg] pointer\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[neg]) at 0x17e1 — dereferences OOB pointer and prints memory as string\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as unsigned: 0 <= index < cookie_num, using both lower-bound and upper-bound checks with unsigned comparison (JB/JAE).\",\"actual\":\"Only `cmp rdx, rax; jl` (signed less-than) at 0x17aa checks upper bound. No lower-bound check exists.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0). Signed comparison JL instead of unsigned JB.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array read + pointer dereference + string output\",\"address\":\"0x17bf (msg OOB read), 0x17e1 (printf %s dereference)\",\"expression\":\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) where index is negative\",\"target\":\"msg[neg] dereferences an OOB pointer from BSS (e.g. stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* at negative offsets)\",\"size\":\"unbounded (printf %s reads until NUL)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"With a negative index, msg[neg] reads a pointer from BSS before msg[] (e.g. stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers). This pointer is dereferenced and its target memory is printed as a string via printf(\\\"%s\\\"), potentially leaking libc addresses and heap addresses from the FILE structure or other memory pointed to by the OOB pointer.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The msg[neg] access itself is an out-of-bounds read from the msg[] array, reading a pointer value from BSS memory that is not part of the intended array bounds.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx, rax; jl 0x17bb — signed JL comparison on uint64 index. No lower-bound check. Negative values pass.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf\",\"description\":\"lea rdx, [rax*8+0x0] then add msg base (0x4060) — uses negative index to compute OOB address before msg[] array, reading an OOB 8-byte pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"call printf@plt with format string %s and OOB pointer as argument — dereferences the OOB pointer and prints memory content as a string.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg[] at 0x4060. Index -8 reaches 0x4020 (stdout FILE*), index -6 reaches 0x4030 (stdin FILE*), index -4 reaches 0x4040 (stderr FILE*). These are valid libc pointers that, when dereferenced and printed as strings, leak libc internal memory.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that OOB FILE* pointers are valid readable addresses at runtime\",\"Verify that printf(%s) on FILE* pointer content produces useful address leakage\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"SIGNED_SIZE_CHECK_BYPASS_IN_CREATE_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"create_cookie reads the message size via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") as a signed int64, then checks `size <= 0x100` using a signed JLE comparison at 0x13f6. A negative value (e.g. -1) passes this check since -1 <= 0x100. The negative size is then passed to calloc(size, 1), which interprets it as a very large unsigned value and returns NULL. The NULL pointer is subsequently passed to read(0, NULL, size), causing a NULL pointer dereference and crash. Additionally, when size=0 is accepted, msg_size is stored as 0, and edit_cookie later computes msg_size[idx]-1 = 0-1 = -1, which is sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, causing a massive heap buffer overflow.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"create_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size[] (0x4160)\",\"heap buffer from calloc\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"message_size via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") in create_cookie (menu choice 2)\",\"entry_function\":\"create_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"scanf(\\\"%lld\\\", &size) at 0x13ed reads signed int64 size\",\"cmp rax, 0x100; jle at 0x13f6 — signed comparison, negative values pass\",\"calloc(size, 1) at 0x1416 — with size=-1, calloc receives 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, returns NULL\",\"read(0, NULL, -1) at 0x1443 — NULL pointer dereference, SIGSEGV\",\"Alternatively: size=0 passes check, calloc(0,1) returns valid pointer, msg_size[idx]=0\",\"Later in edit_cookie: msg_size[idx]-1 = 0-1 = -1 (int32), movsxd extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"read(0, buf, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) — massive heap buffer overflow\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Size should be validated as unsigned and within [1, 0x100] to ensure a meaningful allocation and prevent integer underflow in later size calculations.\",\"actual\":\"Only `cmp rax, 0x100; jle` (signed less-or-equal) at 0x13f6. No lower-bound check (size > 0). Signed comparison allows negative values.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (size > 0 or size >= 1). Signed comparison instead of unsigned. No check for size=0 which causes underflow in edit_cookie's msg_size[idx]-1 calculation.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"NULL pointer dereference via calloc failure + heap buffer overflow via size underflow\",\"address\":\"0x1416 (calloc with negative size), 0x1443 (read to NULL), 0x16bc (movsxd of 0-1 in edit_cookie)\",\"expression\":\"calloc(-1, 1) returns NULL; read(0, NULL, -1) crashes. Or: msg_size[idx]=0, then read(0, buf, (int32)0-1 sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\",\"target\":\"NULL address (crash) or heap buffer (overflow)\",\"size\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (when size=0 and edit_cookie computes 0-1)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"When size=-1 is provided, calloc(-1,1) returns NULL, and read(0, NULL, -1) dereferences the NULL pointer, causing SIGSEGV.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"When size=0 is provided, msg_size[idx] is stored as 0. In edit_cookie, msg_size[idx]-1 = -1 is sign-extended via movsxd to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, and read(0, buf, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) writes an enormous amount of attacker-controlled data past the heap buffer boundary, corrupting adjacent heap metadata and objects.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The heap buffer overflow from the size underflow writes past the allocated buffer (calloc(0,1) returns a minimal allocation), overwriting adjacent heap chunks.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f6\",\"description\":\"cmp rax, 0x100; jle 0x140a — signed JLE comparison. Negative size values (e.g. -1) pass since -1 <= 0x100.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1416\",\"description\":\"call calloc@plt with rdi=size (negative). calloc interprets size as unsigned, receives 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, allocation fails, returns NULL.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1443\",\"description\":\"call read@plt with rsi=NULL (from failed calloc). read(0, NULL, -1) causes NULL pointer dereference.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1498\",\"description\":\"mov ecx, edx — size is truncated from 64-bit to 32-bit when stored in msg_size[]. When size=0, msg_size[idx]=0.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16bc\",\"description\":\"movsxd rdx, eax — sign-extends (msg_size[idx]-1) from 32-bit to 64-bit. When msg_size[idx]=0, result is 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, used as read length.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm calloc(-1,1) returns NULL at runtime\",\"Verify that size=0 followed by edit_cookie produces a heap overflow with 0xFFFFFFFF length\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-004\",\"title\":\"DANGLING_POINTER_IN_EAT_COOKIE_SELF_ASSIGNMENT\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie frees msg[idx] then copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx] (swap-with-last). When idx == cookie_num-1 (the last element), the swap becomes a self-assignment: msg[idx] = msg[idx], which writes the freed pointer back into the same slot. The freed pointer is not NULLed. After cookie_num is decremented, the slot at old index cookie_num-1 is now outside the valid range [0, new_cookie_num-1], but the dangling pointer remains in the msg[] array. If cookie_num later increases (via create_cookie), this slot may become valid again and the dangling pointer can be dereferenced.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[] (0x4060)\",\"freed heap chunk\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_index from fread(/dev/urandom) % cookie_num in eat_cookie (menu choice 1)\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"fread(/dev/urandom) at 0x152b reads random byte\",\"idiv cookie_num at 0x153e computes random_index = urandom_byte % cookie_num\",\"free(msg[random_index]) at 0x158e frees the heap buffer\",\"msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15c6 — when random_index == cookie_num-1, this is self-assignment of freed pointer\",\"msg_size[random_index] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] at 0x15fc — self-assignment of size\",\"cookie_num -= 1 at 0x160a — slot is now outside valid range\",\"Dangling pointer remains in msg[old_cookie_num-1], not NULLed\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After freeing msg[idx], the pointer should be set to NULL (msg[idx] = NULL) to prevent dangling pointer use. Alternatively, the self-assignment case should be handled specially.\",\"actual\":\"No NULLing of freed pointer. The swap-with-last logic does not check for the self-assignment case (idx == cookie_num-1).\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing NULL assignment after free. Missing self-assignment guard in swap-with-last logic.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"free + dangling pointer retention\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free), 0x15c6 (self-assignment of freed pointer)\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[idx]); msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; // when idx==cookie_num-1, freed pointer is retained\",\"target\":\"msg[idx] retains freed heap pointer\",\"size\":\"8 bytes (pointer)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The dangling pointer in msg[] may become accessible again if cookie_num increases (via create_cookie reusing the slot). Subsequent edit_cookie or read_cookie operations on this slot would dereference the freed pointer, potentially accessing reallocated heap memory (use-after-free).\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed heap memory has been reclaimed or unmapped, dereferencing the dangling pointer causes a crash.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If read_cookie is called on the dangling slot (after cookie_num increases to re-include it), printf(\\\"%s\\\", freed_ptr) may leak heap metadata or data from the reallocated chunk.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"call free@plt with rdi=msg[random_index] — frees the heap buffer at the random index.\"},{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15c6\",\"description\":\"mov QWORD PTR [rcx+rdx*1], rax — writes msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index]. When random_index == cookie_num-1, this is a self-assignment that writes the freed pointer back into the same slot.\"},{\"id\":\"E017\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x160a\",\"description\":\"mov QWORD PTR [rip+0x2bd7], rax — decrements cookie_num. The slot at old cookie_num-1 is now outside [0, new_cookie_num-1] but retains the freed pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E018\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15c6\",\"description\":\"No NULL assignment follows the free. The swap logic unconditionally copies msg[cookie_num-1] to msg[idx] without checking idx == cookie_num-1, leaving the freed pointer dangling in the array.\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that the dangling slot can become valid again when create_cookie increments cookie_num\",\"Verify whether the random index can reliably hit cookie_num-1 (probability 1/cookie_num per call)\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"Upper bound check on cookie_num (cookie_num <= 0x1f) and size upper bound (size <= 0x100) are present and correct for their intended purpose.\",\"reason\":\"The upper-bound checks are correct for preventing array overflow of msg[32] and limiting allocation size. The vulnerability is in the missing lower bound, not the upper bound.\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"Menu switch on menu_choice 1-5 with default loop-back.\",\"reason\":\"The switch correctly handles only choices 1-5 and loops back for invalid input. No memory operations in main itself.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"Pre-allocates 5 cookies via strdup with fixed sizes.\",\"reason\":\"strdup allocations are from read-only string literals. No user input involved. The urandom != NULL check is correct.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether CET (IBT/SHSTK) is enabled at runtime, which would affect the practical impact of control-flow corruption\",\"reason\":\"checksec does not report CET flags; kernel/config dependent\",\"required_next_step\":\"Runtime CET detection via /proc/cpuinfo flags or arch_prctl\"},{\"description\":\"Exact heap layout and tcache behavior under glibc 2.39 for the allocation sizes used\",\"reason\":\"Requires dynamic heap analysis to determine chunk alignment and tcache bin placement\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic heap analysis with GDB to observe tcache state after eat_cookie free operations\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie dangling pointer is practically reachable given the random index selection\",\"reason\":\"Index is random from /dev/urandom; triggering idx==cookie_num-1 requires 1/cookie_num probability per call\",\"required_next_step\":\"Statistical analysis or repeated execution to confirm trigger probability\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":3,\"verified\":3,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":0,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"edit_cookie reads index via scanf('%llu') as uint64, checks index < cookie_num using signed JL at 0x1681 with no lower-bound check. Negative index (large uint64) passes check, used in LEA scaling to access memory before msg[] and msg_size[] arrays.\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie(-8) uses msg[-8] = stdout FILE* (0x4020) as write destination for read(0, stdout_ptr, msg_size[-8]-1). The OOB pointer (stdout FILE*) is a valid writable libc address, so attacker-controlled data is written to the stdout FILE structure.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index check\",\"Signed JL comparison allows negative uint64 index to pass\",\"msg[-8] reads stdout FILE* pointer from BSS at 0x4020 (offset -8 from msg base 0x4060)\",\"read(0, msg[-8], msg_size[-8]-1) writes attacker data to stdout FILE structure\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE: write to fixed target (stdout FILE struct) with attacker-controlled content\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write content (data sent to read()), write destination (stdout FILE struct via OOB pointer)\",\"how\":\"edit_cookie with index -8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 as uint64) passes signed JL check; msg[-8] = stdout FILE* at BSS 0x4020; read(0, stdout_ptr, msg_size[-8]-1) writes attacker bytes to FILE struct\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Negative index -8 (uint64 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) must pass signed JL comparison against cookie_num (5). Since -8 < 5 in signed comparison, this passes.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to reach OOB memory before msg[] array\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-8] must contain a valid writable pointer. At runtime, stdout FILE* at BSS 0x4020 is initialized by libc to point to _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc's writable data segment.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for read() to succeed (write destination must be writable)\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-8] must be a small positive value so read length (msg_size[-8]-1) is reasonable. msg_size[-8] is at 0x4140 = low 4 bytes of msg[28]. After creating 24 cookies (cookie_num 5->29), msg[28] contains a heap pointer. Low 4 bytes of heap pointer (e.g. 0x55559700) is a large positive number, so read length is large but read() returns actual bytes read.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for read() to not fail with EFAULT due to negative/huge length\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-8] = *(int32*)(0x4160 + (-8)*4) = *(int32*)0x4140\",\"alias\":\"Low 4 bytes of msg[28] (at 0x4140, since msg[28] spans 0x4140-0x4147)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-8] aliases the low 4 bytes of msg[28]. After creating 24 cookies, msg[28] has a heap pointer. Low 4 bytes = large positive (e.g. 0x55559700), so read length = 0x55559700-1 = large but valid. read() returns actual bytes read, not full length.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run poc-BUG-001.py which creates 24 cookies then calls edit_cookie(-8) with 8 bytes of data. Verify program stays alive after write (stdout FILE struct corrupted but no immediate crash).\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Program alive after edit_cookie(-8) write to stdout FILE struct\",\"GDB confirms write destination = stdout FILE* (valid libc address)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on RELATIVE_WRITE to FILE struct. RELRO protects GOT, not FILE structures.\"},{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes BSS addresses but msg[-8] always points to stdout FILE* at fixed PIE-relative offset. No impact on primitive validity.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on data write primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on heap/BSS write primitive.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692 — signed JL comparison on uint64 index. No lower-bound check. -8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) passes since -8 < 5.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3\",\"description\":\"lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0] then add msg base (0x4060) — index -8 computes address 0x4060-64=0x4020 = stdout FILE* in BSS.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"call read@plt with rsi=msg[-8]=stdout FILE* — writes attacker data to stdout FILE structure.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB confirmed: edit_cookie(-8) calls read(0, stdout_FILE_ptr, msg_size[-8]-1). rsi=stdout FILE* (valid libc address). Program alive after write, confirming successful write to stdout FILE struct.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0x4242424242424242\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"edit_cookie(-8) writes 8 bytes (0x4242424242424242) to stdout FILE structure. Program remains alive after write. GDB confirmed rsi = stdout FILE* (valid writable libc address). This is RELATIVE_WRITE (fixed target = stdout FILE struct, not attacker-controlled address).\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"This is RELATIVE_WRITE, not ARB_WRITE: the write destination (stdout FILE struct) is determined by the OOB index (-8), not by attacker choice. The attacker controls the write content but not the write target address. Upgraded to ARB_WRITE via PRIM-002 two-step write.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"INDIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"edit_cookie reads index via scanf('%llu') as uint64, checks index < cookie_num using signed JL at 0x1681 with no lower-bound check. Negative index passes check, accessing OOB memory before msg[] and msg_size[].\",\"description\":\"Two-step ARB_WRITE via __dso_handle self-reference: (1) edit_cookie(-11) writes target_addr Y to __dso_handle (self-referencing pointer at 0x4008 = msg[-11]); (2) edit_cookie(-11) again now writes to *Y since msg[-11]=Y. This upgrades from RELATIVE_WRITE to ARB_WRITE.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index check\",\"Signed JL allows negative index -11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) to pass\",\"msg[-11] = __dso_handle at 0x4008, which is self-referencing (value == &value == PIE_base+0x4008)\",\"Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) → read(0, &__dso_handle, size) overwrites __dso_handle with attacker-chosen target address Y\",\"Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) again → now msg[-11]=Y, so read(0, Y, size) writes attacker data to arbitrary address Y\",\"ARB_WRITE: two-step write achieves Write-What-Where (attacker controls both target address and value)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write target address (Y, written to __dso_handle in step 1) AND write content (data written to *Y in step 2)\",\"how\":\"Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) with 8-byte payload = target_addr Y → read(0, __dso_handle, size) writes Y to __dso_handle. Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) with 8-byte payload = value → read(0, Y, size) writes value to Y.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Negative index -11 (uint64 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) must pass signed JL comparison against cookie_num. Since -11 < 5 in signed comparison, this passes.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to reach __dso_handle at msg[-11] = 0x4008\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"__dso_handle at 0x4008 must be self-referencing (value == &value). GDB confirmed: at runtime, *(PIE_base+0x4008) = PIE_base+0x4008. This is the key enabler for two-step write.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for step 1 to write to __dso_handle itself (not to some other address). Self-reference means read(0, __dso_handle, size) writes to __dso_handle's own address.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-11] must be a small positive value so read length is valid. msg_size[-11] at 0x4134 = high 4 bytes of msg[26]. After creating 22 cookies (cookie_num 5->27), msg[26] contains a heap pointer (e.g. 0x0000555555559XXX). High 4 bytes = 0x00005555 = 21845 (small positive). Read length = 21845-1 = 21844, which is valid.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for read() to succeed. If msg_size[-11]=0, read length = 0-1 = -1 → 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF → read returns -1 (EFAULT).\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"After step 1, __dso_handle = Y (target address). For step 2 to succeed, Y must be a valid writable address. In verification, Y = msg[0] address (PIE_base+0x4060, writable BSS).\",\"necessity\":\"Required for step 2 read() to succeed (write destination must be writable)\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"After step 2, program checks *(msg[-11] + read_ret) for newline. Since msg[-11]=Y and read_ret=8, it accesses *(Y+8). Y must be readable at Y+8.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for program to not crash after step 2 (post-read check at 0x1705)\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(int32*)(0x4160 + (-11)*4) = *(int32*)0x4134\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[26] (msg[26] spans 0x4130-0x4137, high 4 bytes at 0x4134)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-11] aliases the HIGH 4 bytes of msg[26]. After creating 22 cookies, msg[26] has a heap pointer like 0x0000555555559XXX. HIGH 4 bytes = 0x00005555 = 21845 (small positive). This is the GOLDEN alias: high half of a 64-bit pointer is always small (0x0000XXXX for user-space addresses), so read length = 21844 is valid. If it aliased the LOW 4 bytes instead, the value would be ~0x55559XXX (large), still positive but very large.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(uint64*)(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(uint64*)0x4008 = __dso_handle\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle global variable at 0x4008 in .data section\",\"implication\":\"__dso_handle is self-referencing: its value equals its own address. This means read(0, __dso_handle, size) writes to __dso_handle's own address, allowing step 1 to modify __dso_handle to any target address Y. Then step 2 writes to *Y. This is the two-step ARB_WRITE upgrade from RELATIVE_WRITE.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run poc-BUG-001.py which: (1) leaks PIE base via read_cookie(-11); (2) creates 22 cookies; (3) step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes target_addr to __dso_handle; (4) step 2: edit_cookie(-11) writes value to target_addr. GDB verifies both steps. Additionally, verify_arb_write.py (deleted, results below) verified two different targets Y1 and Y2.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"GDB: __dso_handle changes from self-referencing value to target_addr after step 1\",\"GDB: msg[0] changes from heap pointer to 0xdeadbeefcafebabe after step 2\",\"Two different targets Y1 (msg[0]) and Y2 (msg[1]) both written successfully in separate runs\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on ARB_WRITE to BSS/data. RELRO protects GOT entries from write, but the two-step write targets BSS (.bss is writable). The primitive writes to arbitrary addresses including BSS, heap, and stack (all writable). GOT is also writable after RELRO is resolved, but Full RELRO makes GOT read-only after relocation.\"},{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes addresses but the primitive leaks PIE base via read_cookie(-11) (__dso_handle self-reference). After leak, all PIE-relative addresses are known. ASLR does not prevent the primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on data write primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on BSS/heap write primitive.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692 — signed JL on uint64 index. -11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) passes since -11 < 5.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3\",\"description\":\"lea rcx,[rax*8] + msg base (0x4060) — index -11 computes 0x4060-88=0x4008 = __dso_handle.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB confirmed: edit_cookie(-11) calls read(0, __dso_handle_addr, msg_size[-11]-1). rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle, self-referencing). rdx=0x5555-1=21844 (valid length from msg[26] high 4 bytes).\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4008\",\"description\":\"GDB after step 1: __dso_handle changed from 0x00005d5c4cafc008 (self-referencing) to 0x00005d5c4cafc060 (target_addr = msg[0]). Confirms step 1 writes target address to __dso_handle.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060\",\"description\":\"GDB after step 2: msg[0] changed from 0x00005d5c70cd7480 (heap pointer) to 0xdeadbeefcafebabe (attacker value). Confirms step 2 writes to target address.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060\",\"description\":\"ARB_WRITE acceptance test: two different targets Y1 (msg[0], offset 0x4060) and Y2 (msg[1], offset 0x4068) both written successfully in separate runs. Y1: 0x00005f918a2b7480 -> 0x1111111111111111. Y2: 0x000061a541da44e0 -> 0x2222222222222222. Both verified via GDB.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0xdeadbeefcafebabe\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"Two-step ARB_WRITE verified via GDB: Step 1: __dso_handle (0x5d5c4cafc008) changed from self-referencing 0x00005d5c4cafc008 to target 0x00005d5c4cafc060 (msg[0]). Step 2: msg[0] changed from heap ptr 0x00005d5c70cd7480 to 0xdeadbeefcafebabe. Additional test with two different targets: Y1 (0x4060) written 0x1111111111111111, Y2 (0x4068) written 0x2222222222222222, both GDB-verified.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"ARB_WRITE is INDIRECT (two-step via __dso_handle self-reference). The self-referencing property of __dso_handle is the key enabler: it allows step 1 to modify the pointer used in step 2. This upgrades RELATIVE_WRITE (fixed target via OOB index) to ARB_WRITE (attacker-chosen target via two-step indirection). The alias msg_size[-11] = high 4 bytes of msg[26] is critical: after creating 22 cookies, msg[26] has a heap pointer whose high 4 bytes (0x00005555) provide a valid small-positive read length.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"read_cookie reads index via scanf('%llu') as uint64, checks index < cookie_num using signed JL at 0x17aa with no lower-bound check. Negative index passes check, accessing OOB memory before msg[] array. The OOB pointer value is dereferenced and printed as string via printf('%s').\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-8) uses msg[-8] = stdout FILE* (0x4020) as argument to printf('%s'), leaking the first bytes of the stdout FILE structure (libc internal memory). read_cookie(-11) uses msg[-11] = __dso_handle (0x4008, self-referencing) to leak a PIE address, defeating ASLR.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index check (same pattern in read_cookie)\",\"Signed JL allows negative index -8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) to pass\",\"msg[-8] = stdout FILE* at BSS 0x4020 (offset -8 from msg base 0x4060)\",\"printf('%s', msg[-8]) dereferences stdout FILE* and prints FILE struct content as string\",\"INFO_LEAK: leaks libc internal memory (stdout _flags = 0xfbad2887) and PIE base (via __dso_handle self-reference at index -11)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Leaked information: stdout FILE struct content (libc addresses) and __dso_handle value (PIE base address)\",\"how\":\"read_cookie with negative index -8 or -11. Index -8: msg[-8]=stdout FILE*, printf('%s') leaks FILE struct bytes until NUL. Index -11: msg[-11]=__dso_handle (self-referencing), printf('%s') leaks PIE address bytes until NUL.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Negative index -8 or -11 (as uint64) must pass signed JL comparison against cookie_num. Since -8 < 5 and -11 < 5 in signed comparison, both pass.\",\"necessity\":\"Required to reach OOB memory before msg[] array\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-8] must contain a valid readable pointer (stdout FILE*). At runtime, stdout is initialized by libc to point to _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc's readable data segment.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for printf('%s') to dereference and print without crashing\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg[-11] must contain a valid readable pointer (__dso_handle, self-referencing). At runtime, __dso_handle = &__dso_handle = PIE_base+0x4008, which is in readable .data section.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for printf('%s') to dereference and print PIE address\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(uint64*)(0x4060 + (-8)*8) = *(uint64*)0x4020 = stdout FILE*\",\"alias\":\"stdout global variable at BSS 0x4020\",\"implication\":\"msg[-8] reads the stdout FILE* pointer from BSS. This pointer points to _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc. printf('%s') on this pointer leaks the FILE structure content (first bytes until NUL), including _flags = 0xfbad2887.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(uint64*)(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(uint64*)0x4008 = __dso_handle\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle global variable at .data 0x4008 (self-referencing)\",\"implication\":\"msg[-11] reads __dso_handle, which is self-referencing (value == &value == PIE_base+0x4008). printf('%s') on this pointer prints the bytes of the address itself (since the pointer points to itself), leaking 6 bytes of PIE address (stops at NUL byte in high bytes).\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run poc-BUG-001.py which calls read_cookie(-8) and read_cookie(-11), capturing leaked bytes.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"read_cookie(-8) leaks stdout _flags = 0xfbad2887 (4 bytes, libc internal value)\",\"read_cookie(-11) leaks __dso_handle = PIE_base+0x4008 (6 bytes, PIE address)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes addresses but read_cookie(-11) leaks __dso_handle (self-referencing PIE address), defeating PIE. After leak, PIE base is known.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on read primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on read primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on read primitive.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x17bb — signed JL on uint64 index. -8 and -11 pass since both < 5 in signed comparison.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*8] + msg base (0x4060) — index -8 computes 0x4020 (stdout), index -11 computes 0x4008 (__dso_handle).\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"call printf@plt with format %s and OOB pointer — dereferences OOB pointer and prints memory content as string.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-8) leaked 4 bytes: 0xfbad2887 = _IO_2_1_stdout_._flags (libc internal value, not attacker-provided). Confirms INFO_LEAK from stdout FILE structure.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-11) leaked 6 bytes of __dso_handle value = PIE_base+0x4008. PIE base computed as 0x00005b7624b41000 (page-aligned, valid). Confirms PIE base leak defeating ASLR.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0xfbad2887 (stdout _flags, 4 bytes) and 0x00005b7624b45008 (__dso_handle, 6 bytes -> PIE base 0x00005b7624b41000)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"read_cookie(-8) leaks stdout FILE struct: _flags=0xfbad2887 (4 bytes, stops at NUL in _IO_read_ptr). read_cookie(-11) leaks __dso_handle self-referencing value: 6 bytes -> PIE base = leaked_value - 0x4008 (page-aligned). Multiple runs confirm stable leak.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK is DIRECT: the OOB pointer is directly dereferenced by printf('%s'), leaking memory content. Two leak channels: (1) stdout FILE struct (libc internal data, _flags=0xfbad2887); (2) __dso_handle (PIE address, defeats ASLR). The __dso_handle leak is critical for PRIM-002 ARB_WRITE (needs PIE base to compute target addresses).\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"CRASH\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"edit_cookie(-1) accesses msg[-1] at 0x4058 (gap between stderr and msg[], value=0). read(0, NULL, ...) returns -1 (EFAULT). Program does not crash immediately but read returns -1. This is a failed write path, not a useful primitive.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-002\",\"candidate_type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"Indices -4 (stderr), -6 (stdin) also reach FILE* pointers and can write to FILE structures (same as PRIM-001 with stdout